WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:03.529
In this video, I want to briefly cover

2
00:00:03.529 --> 00:00:07.349
a new experimental feature within Unreal Engine that

3
00:00:07.349 --> 00:00:08.970
is the day sequence.

4
00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.609
So let's jump over to the editor and take a look.

5
00:00:11.609 --> 00:00:15.369
So I'm going to start with the litatmos in class map

6
00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:17.370
within our content maps folder.

7
00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.089
Now, if you've been following along

8
00:00:19.089 --> 00:00:21.170
with previous videos and you still

9
00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.670
have your old lighting or the lighting

10
00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.469
that we created in previous videos,

11
00:00:25.469 --> 00:00:28.050
go ahead and just delete all those actors.

12
00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.390
And we can keep our post-processing volume.

13
00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:31.929
That's absolutely fine.

14
00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:33.509
So we shouldn't have any other lighting

15
00:00:33.509 --> 00:00:35.909
except our small accent lights.

16
00:00:35.909 --> 00:00:39.030
The rest of all of our day and atmosphere components

17
00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.289
should be completely gone.

18
00:00:41.289 --> 00:00:43.170
So I'm going to go over to our place actors.

19
00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.369
And I'm just going to type in sun.

20
00:00:45.369 --> 00:00:47.770
And I'm going to look for sun moon.

21
00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.689
Let's go ahead and drag this into our scene.

22
00:00:50.689 --> 00:00:52.189
And you'll notice that we already

23
00:00:52.189 --> 00:00:54.609
start to get some lighting within here.

24
00:00:54.609 --> 00:00:57.649
So let me just cover real quick what this is.

25
00:00:57.649 --> 00:01:01.090
So the sun moon day sequence actor

26
00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:06.170
is a component that's built on essentially blueprints

27
00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:10.010
and some code with all of the various different components

28
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.269
that we've seen before when we were lighting

29
00:01:12.269 --> 00:01:13.730
our scene from scratch.

30
00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.329
So here we have our directional light.

31
00:01:15.329 --> 00:01:17.189
Now, in this case, we have one for our sun

32
00:01:17.189 --> 00:01:19.189
and one for our moon.

33
00:01:19.189 --> 00:01:21.950
Our exponential height fog, sky atmosphere,

34
00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:24.150
our sky light, volumetric clouds,

35
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:27.590
and then a sky sphere for projecting on the back.

36
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:30.469
All of these are controlled through various different route

37
00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:33.430
components and the blueprint logic

38
00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:36.510
that will allow us to essentially mimic

39
00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.450
an actual time of day.

40
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.069
Now, real quick, what I'm going to do

41
00:01:40.069 --> 00:01:43.469
is I'm actually going to rotate this 75 degrees, which

42
00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:46.909
should allow us to get some light kind of going in here.

43
00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:50.230
And you can see that I can adjust my daytime preview.

44
00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:52.549
I'm just going to scrub this real quick.

45
00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:56.390
Now, one quick caveat why I rotated this 75 degrees.

46
00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:00.150
So typically speaking, if you were using this in production,

47
00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.510
now another caveat, I would not recommend

48
00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:05.719
using this in production because this is still experimental.

49
00:02:05.719 --> 00:02:08.400
But if you had this in your scene,

50
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.759
ideally you would have your scene oriented with essentially

51
00:02:12.759 --> 00:02:16.939
would be x forward, would kind of be your main direction.

52
00:02:16.939 --> 00:02:21.319
But because this particular, our assets that are built here

53
00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:23.599
are rotated and I want to get the light going through here,

54
00:02:23.599 --> 00:02:27.120
I've just simply rotated the whole actor

55
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.719
by 75 degrees, which allows me to get some light there.

56
00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:31.719
So that's the only reason why I did that.

57
00:02:31.719 --> 00:02:34.120
It doesn't have to do with anything else.

58
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:39.729
All right, so at the heart of this actor

59
00:02:39.729 --> 00:02:40.849
is this time of day.

60
00:02:40.849 --> 00:02:43.449
So you can see as I scrub this, so I can just kind of drag

61
00:02:43.449 --> 00:02:45.770
this little preview here, you'll

62
00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:49.610
notice that it's essentially based off of a 24-hour clock.

63
00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:52.889
And what this is doing is this is just dynamically

64
00:02:52.889 --> 00:02:56.370
adjusting our light, so all those components,

65
00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.490
as if we were doing it manually,

66
00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.889
well, we can run this over and over again.

67
00:03:00.889 --> 00:03:02.210
Now, hopefully this will work.

68
00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:05.449
But if you press Play, this should start ticking

69
00:03:05.449 --> 00:03:06.370
and it should work.

70
00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:10.050
You may notice, you may not see it updating.

71
00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:13.210
But this is essentially running a full time of day cycle.

72
00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:14.849
Let me go ahead and pause it.

73
00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:18.050
So what I want to show is a couple other different features

74
00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.569
that actually make this work.

75
00:03:19.569 --> 00:03:21.409
But what I want to do first is just

76
00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:24.370
update or change one of these properties

77
00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.490
so that this time of day updates a lot faster.

78
00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:33.099
So I would do that through our, it should be, I believe,

79
00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:37.340
our time of day, time per cycle.

80
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.099
And this is basically saying, how long

81
00:03:39.099 --> 00:03:41.340
is it going to take in actual units

82
00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.780
for a full time of day cycle to complete?

83
00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.740
So right now, it's set at five minutes.

84
00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:50.939
So going from 0, 100 hours all the way through to 0,

85
00:03:50.939 --> 00:03:53.460
100 hours again is going to take five minutes.

86
00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:56.460
So if I adjust this down to say something like 30 seconds,

87
00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:00.330
should be fast, hit Enter, hit Tab.

88
00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.490
If I press Play, again, this is a little bug

89
00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:04.689
that I've noticed right now.

90
00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:06.889
Again, fully experimental, that it still

91
00:04:06.889 --> 00:04:09.530
is updating at our same interval.

92
00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:11.090
So to change that, all I'm going to do

93
00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:14.810
is just go up to our Play settings here.

94
00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:16.810
And instead of selected viewport,

95
00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:18.209
I'm just going to do Simulate.

96
00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:20.009
So if I do Simulate, we should see it.

97
00:04:20.009 --> 00:04:20.730
And there we go.

98
00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.810
Now it's, again, rotating as fast as I want.

99
00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:27.610
30 seconds to go through a full time of day cycle.

100
00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:31.240
Let's just play for a second so you can see it.

101
00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.639
And one of the other components here is our moon.

102
00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:36.360
So now we're kind of at nighttime.

103
00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:40.540
If I zoom up here, you'll see that we do have our stars.

104
00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:43.360
And in this case, I think it should loop back on us.

105
00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.279
We'll see here, it may just be fixed.

106
00:04:46.279 --> 00:04:49.240
But that is one of the other advantages of using this.

107
00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.360
I'm going to hit stuff.

108
00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:51.680
Oh, that actually is still going.

109
00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:52.279
There we go.

110
00:04:52.279 --> 00:04:53.740
So you can see that this will just

111
00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:55.439
need to loop over and over again

112
00:04:55.439 --> 00:05:00.060
to have a real time time of day, which is a great feature.

113
00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.839
Highly requested for many, many, many engine versions.

114
00:05:04.839 --> 00:05:06.959
All right.

115
00:05:06.959 --> 00:05:10.680
One of the other major components

116
00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:16.000
of this actual system is this day sequence collection.

117
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.600
Now you can add the sequence to this.

118
00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.439
But by default, it adds this DSCA 24 hour.

119
00:05:21.439 --> 00:05:23.600
And you could browse to this, or we can just

120
00:05:23.639 --> 00:05:28.160
simply double click this little icon and open it up.

121
00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.199
This is where, if you are considering

122
00:05:30.199 --> 00:05:32.600
using this in your project, this

123
00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:34.720
is where you're probably going to make a lot of key

124
00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.120
changes.

125
00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.000
So in particular, we have the sequence.

126
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.259
So if I just open this up, this is a movie sequence.

127
00:05:41.259 --> 00:05:43.620
And you see that it does target

128
00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:44.980
a couple of different properties.

129
00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:46.899
So our sky, sphere, volumetric cloud,

130
00:05:46.899 --> 00:05:48.819
and then some of the properties through here.

131
00:05:48.819 --> 00:05:51.660
So things such as sun intensity, moon size,

132
00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:56.160
stars intensity, you can adjust that here in the sequence.

133
00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.819
Let me close the sequencer.

134
00:05:58.819 --> 00:06:01.459
The other thing that we have here is this sun position

135
00:06:01.459 --> 00:06:04.449
sequence within the procedural sequences.

136
00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:06.370
Now this is probably the most important one.

137
00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:08.930
So if you're familiar with long lat, so longitude,

138
00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:11.209
latitude, and time zones, this

139
00:06:11.209 --> 00:06:14.009
is where you can essentially mimic

140
00:06:14.009 --> 00:06:16.930
how the sun and the moon would interact based off

141
00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:19.089
of a physical location.

142
00:06:19.089 --> 00:06:23.250
So in this case, we're negative 5 GMT, latitude

143
00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:24.370
and longitude.

144
00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:26.660
You can enable daylight savings time.

145
00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.740
And all this is going to do is just simply give you

146
00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:34.139
a more realistic or real world based type of system.

147
00:06:34.139 --> 00:06:37.740
So again, you could just type in real quick to Google Maps,

148
00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:41.060
find a location, find the long lat, plug those in.

149
00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:44.019
And as your day sequence run, it's

150
00:06:44.019 --> 00:06:48.459
going to look more natural like that actual location.

151
00:06:48.459 --> 00:06:52.500
So again, this is located within the day sequence collection.

152
00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:54.860
If you expand it out and just double click this,

153
00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:56.220
that will open those settings.

154
00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:58.899
Now I would recommend if we just quickly browse to it,

155
00:06:58.899 --> 00:07:01.379
you'll see here it's actually in the engine content.

156
00:07:01.379 --> 00:07:05.939
Copy this DSCA 24 hours, this day sequence collection,

157
00:07:05.939 --> 00:07:07.899
put it in your local project folder,

158
00:07:07.899 --> 00:07:09.860
and rename it to something else.

159
00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:12.899
That way you can keep the original engine version

160
00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:17.050
and you have your own that you could add to your project.

161
00:07:17.089 --> 00:07:18.730
So again, that's a really cool feature

162
00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:21.529
that's in there called the sun, moon, day sequence actor.

163
00:07:21.529 --> 00:07:23.610
But again, caveat, it is still experimental.

164
00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:26.050
So use at your own risk.

165
00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:30.560
But it is a great new feature making its way into the engine.

166
00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:33.160
In the next video, we'll discuss the new MegaLights

167
00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:36.079
feature included in Unreal Engine.
